  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Radioactive decay is a random and spontaneous process, it depends on unbalancing of nucleus.&nbsp;This process is dependent on the law of conservation of charge.</p><p>We know that,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mfenced><mn>1</mn></mfenced></math>,&nbsp;Where, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>N</mi></math>=number of decayed nuclei in the sample at time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>t</mi></math>&nbsp;and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo></math>initial number of nuclei.</p><p>Hence, total number of undecayed nuclei&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>-</mo><mi>N</mi></math></p><p>Thus,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>-</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mfenced><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>-</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mfenced></math></p><p>This shows that the total number of undecayed nuclei decays exponentially with time.&nbsp;</p><p>Taking log both sides of equation&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mn>1</mn></mfenced></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>ln</mi><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ln</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math></p><p>So, slope&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mi>&#955;</mi></math>&nbsp; (reciprocal of mean life time)</p><p>Half life time&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>T</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>&#955;</mi></mfrac></math></p><p>Then,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#955;</mi><mo>&#215;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>ln</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>constant</mi></math></p><p>Hence, (B) and (C) are correct only.</p>",
    "image": null
  },
